# PINGO — web environment
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values from your Supabase project:
#   Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Never commit real credentials.
#
# Note on the anon key: it is safe to expose in a browser bundle by design —
# it only grants what Row Level Security policies allow. The service_role key
# is NOT safe and must never appear in this file or anywhere in apps/web.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Google Sign-In on Android.
#
# This is the **Web** OAuth client ID, not the Android one — and that trips
# everybody up once. The Android client authorises the app itself, matched on
# package name and signing certificate; it holds no secret and is never named
# in code. But Supabase only accepts an ID token issued for *its* client, so
# the native sign-in asks Google for a token addressed to the web client and
# delivers it to an app already proven to be ours.
#
# Google Cloud → APIs & Services → Credentials → the "Web application" client.
# It must be the same one configured under Supabase → Authentication → Google.
#
# Web builds ignore this entirely; they use the OAuth redirect.
VITE_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
